<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gracchi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gracchi brothers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gracchi were the brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-gracchus">Gaius Sempronius Gracchus</a>, tribunes of the plebs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 133 and 123–122 BCE. They proposed laws to redistribute public land to poor citizens and other reforms, and both were killed with many supporters by their opponents in the Senate. Their deaths are often seen as the start of the violence that destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gracchi.jpg" alt="Gracchi" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Baptiste Claude Eugène Guillaume · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eugene_Guillaume_-_the_Gracchi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus was born about 163 BCE and his younger brother Gaius about 154 BCE. Their father, also Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus, had been consul in 177 and 163 BCE and censor in 169 BCE. Their mother Cornelia was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a>, and their sister Sempronia married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus">Scipio Aemilianus</a>. Tiberius began his military career in 147 BCE under Scipio Aemilianus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, where he was among the first to scale the walls.</p><p>In 137 BCE, as quaestor under the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-hostilius-mancinus">Gaius Hostilius Mancinus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a>, he negotiated a treaty of surrender after a failed campaign in the Numantine War. The senate rejected it humiliatingly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> later attributed his reforms to an attempt to win back standing after that episode, while favourable sources credit spirited advocacy for the poor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>' land law as tribune in 133 BCE had three aims: a commission to survey the public land, a limit of about 500 jugera for any one possessor, and distribution of the remainder to poor Roman citizens, with Italians excluded. It was meant to stimulate population growth and enlarge the number of men qualified for army service. The commission distributed more than 3,268 square kilometres in its first few years. Tiberius was killed in 133 BCE, and the consular commission of 132 BCE passed sentences of exile on his supporters.</p><p>Gaius, elected tribune in the elections of 124 BCE, presented himself as the heir of his brother's programme, but most of his proposals did not concern land. He extended the right of provocatio to the sentences passed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>' supporters, and his lex repetundarum created an equestrian jury to try senatorial governors for provincial corruption. Both brothers were killed during or shortly after their tribunician terms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> is widely viewed as the start of the late republic. Jürgen von Ungern-Sternberg writes that his assassination made 133 BCE a turning point and the beginning of the crisis of the Republic, since the use of violence against political enemies then became acceptable. Modern historians stress that archaeology does not support the ancient picture of a collapsing Italian peasantry, and that most of the brothers' legislation was not repealed.</p><p>Ancient sources are late and mostly follow the narrative of Gaius and his supporters, so what is known of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> is filtered through his brother. Later ages saw them as martyrs or demagogues. The revolutionary François-Noël Babeuf took the name Gracchus in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, though modern scholars regard comparisons with socialism as unapt.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gracchi brothers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
